{"qid":"Q170604","names":["New France"],"title":"New France","endonym":{"native":"Vice-royauté de Nouvelle-France","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"New France was the French colonial domain in North America, originating with Jacques Cartier's exploration of the St. Lawrence in 1534 and taking permanent form with Samuel de Champlain's founding of Quebec City in 1608. At its maximum extent in 1712, it comprised five administrative colonies—Canada, Acadia, Terre-Neuve, Hudson Bay, and Louisiana—stretching from Newfoundland to the Gulf of Mexico and from Hudson Bay to the Rockies, though effective French settlement remained concentrated along the St. Lawrence corridor. The Treaty of Utrecht (1713) ceded mainland Acadia, Hudson Bay, and Newfoundland to Britain, and the Seven Years' War culminated in the Treaty of Paris (1763) by which France surrendered virtually all remaining mainland territory east of the Mississippi to Britain and Louisiana to Spain. French colonial jurisdiction over the remaining Caribbean islands persisted, but New France as a North American continental entity ceased to exist in 1763.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The St. Lawrence core settlements are precisely documented; the claimed territorial extent of Louisiana and the interior is based on exploration routes and nominal claims, and the polygon boundaries are approximate for vast interior regions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: New France","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/New-France"},{"label":"Wikipedia: New France","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_France"}],"britannica":"place/New-France","cowCode":null,"